   "canonical_t1": 1
  },
  "how": "The library's 31 workers are created in OpenBLAS's constructor at load and are alive until the process exits even at -t 1 (measured with ps -L during a 2 s spin after the product). The tensor pool's 15 workers are created on the first split and joined on resize or exit. With B the whole-work library product splits rows over that same pool, so it is the 16th thread and no other: strace counts 15 clones at -t 16, and the thread_pools case pins the live count at 16.",
  "rss_kb_import_t1": {
   "before": [
    20992,
    20992,
    20480
   ],
   "b": [
    20736,
    20224,
    20736
   ]
  },
  "rss_kb_product_t1": {
   "before": 35584,
   "after": 34560
  },
  "user_set": "OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=8 at -t 1 leaves 8 live threads (7 library workers and the caller) during a 2 s spin after a product, measured with ps -L; the seed leaves a user value alone and openblas_set_num_threads(1) does not join the constructor pool. The GEMM still runs one thread per call, so those workers are idle. The single-pool promise is conditional on the variable being unset; the unset run has 1 live thread at -t 1.",
  "rss_note": "The 31 threads cost about 1 MB, the touched page of each stack and bookkeeping, not the 8 MB each stack reserves: what they spend is cores. B creates the same tensor pool at -t 16 as any other split, so its product RSS adds nothing to the pool's."
 },
