 "reading": {
  "problem": "Two pools in one process: the tensor pool spends -t, and OpenBLAS's pool was created in a constructor at load (31 workers here) and given the run's width per call. On a loaded host the second pool spins between calls and competes.",
  "change": "The library runs one thread per call and the tensor pool keeps -t. A whole-work product splits its output rows over the pool, one library call per chunk; K is never split, so every output element's sum stays inside one call. The pool is seeded to 1 for OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS/MKL_NUM_THREADS when the user has not set them, so the constructor cannot create the second pool at all.",
  "curve_says": "The cells A lost are recovered: 2048x1024x1024 library -34.8% at t=8 and -54.0% at t=16 (null 32.9% and 22.3%), 2048x1024x2048 -35.5% at t=16 (null 7.5%) and -52.4% at t=32, 1024x2048x1024 -35% to -81% from t=2 up, 1024^3 -44% to -77% from t=4 up. At t=1 nothing changes (+1.5% at 1024^3, within the null): one chunk is the old one call. The batched cells do not change (the batch loop already spread them): 4x1024^3 library within 3% at every t. The canonical kernel is the same code in both builds; its reads (2048x1024x2048 -26%/-28% at t=16/32, 4x1024^3 +11%/+16% at t=4/8) are the seed removing 31 idle library threads on some cells and the host on others, not a kernel change.",
  "step_says": "d=1024: library 5,076 -> 2,969 ms (-41.5%, null 15.5%) - read; the before copies span 4.6-8.8 s, B 2.0-2.7 s. Canonical is the same kernel in both builds and moves +2.4% (null 0.6%); at d=256 canonical reads -28.0% (null 20.6%) while its library arm (below the 2^30 threshold, so canonical too) reads -2.0% (null 31.2%). Two opposite reads on unchanged code say the step null understates this host window; the arm the change touches is the d=1024 library one, and it reads with the change's size.",
  "threads": "strace: 0 clones for an import at -t 1, 15 for a -t 16 library product (the tensor pool, caller included 16); thread_pools.cml pins 1/4/8/16. The seed is conditional: a user-set OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=8 leaves 8 live threads at -t 1, and the GEMM still runs one thread per call.",
  "licence": "Row blocks only, never K. Canonical: all 42 cells carry the same checksum as the base. Library: 22 of 42 cells equal A's one-call bits; the other 20 differ by at most 3.8e-8 relative (a few 1-ulp elements in a 1M-element f32 tensor), and the library's result again depends on -t (6 checksums per shape, as the base did), because OpenBLAS's own grouping depends on the call's M. The library path already runs under the default reassociation licence; #strict/safe_fp withhold it; the seed touches no sum. The expectation that a row split would be bit-identical to one call is false for this library, and the JSON records it.",
  "next": "If fixed library bits across -t are required, the row split is not the design: the options are A (one thread, fixed bits, 1.6-2.9x slower on wide products) or a canonical path. If one pool and the library's parallelism both are, B is the one that has them, and the licence covers its regrouping."
 },
